# Trivy accepted-risk list.
#
# The CI gate (.github/workflows/ci-cd.yml -> "Security Scan") fails the build on CRITICAL
# findings. Everything listed here is a CRITICAL that has no fix available on the dependency
# line this project is on, with the reasoning recorded so the decision can be re-checked later
# rather than silently inherited.
#
# HIGH findings are intentionally NOT gated and NOT listed here - they still surface in the
# SARIF report uploaded to the repository's Security tab. Most of them trace back to
# Spring Boot 3.2.0 / Camunda 7.20.0 being a major version behind, which is a deliberate
# post-defense upgrade rather than a patch-level fix.

# CVE-2026-22732 - spring-security-web: HTTP response headers may not be written for servlet
# applications, so configured security headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, HSTS) can silently fail
# to apply. That weakens defense-in-depth against clickjacking/XSS; it is not an authentication
# or authorization bypass on its own.
#
# Not fixable here: the OSS fixes are 6.5.9 and 7.0.4. The 6.2.x line this project runs on
# (pinned by spring-boot-starter-parent 3.2.0) ended OSS support at 6.2.8, which is already
# applied in backend/pom.xml; 6.3.15 / 6.4.15 are Enterprise Support-only releases. Reaching
# 6.5.9 means Spring Boot 3.2 -> 3.5, which in turn requires a Camunda 7.20 -> 7.23+ upgrade.
# That upgrade chain is tracked as post-freeze work, not a patch.
CVE-2026-22732

# CVE-2026-47884 - spring-webmvc: remote code execution through improper path limitation in
# XsltView. Only applications that render views with XsltView (XSLT templates) are exposed.
#
# Not reachable here: SupportFlow is a pure REST/JSON API and never uses XsltView or any XSLT
# view resolver (no XsltView / XsltViewResolver anywhere in backend/src). Not fixable on this
# line either: the only fixed release is 7.0.9 (Spring Framework 7 = Spring Boot 4), the same
# post-freeze upgrade chain as CVE-2026-22732 above. Re-check if XSLT views are ever added.
CVE-2026-47884
